@@ -0,0 +1,120 @@
+# Snapshot persistence and read API
+
+The persistence service requests complete collector snapshots instead of merging
+partial node events. It stores one current document in PostgreSQL, atomically.
+The document contains `nodes` and `charts`; `observed_at` records when its node
+response reached the writer. This is dashboard observation data, not chain
+finality or a claim that every node recently sent a report.
+
+## Provisioning
+
+Create a separate database and service roles through your normal PostgreSQL
+administration procedure. Connect explicitly to that database as its schema
+administrator and apply:
+
+```sh
+psql --dbname='<explicit-dashboard-database>' -X -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f db/schema/001-dashboard.sql
+```
+
+This transaction creates `dashboard_v1.snapshot`; it neither drops nor rewrites
+existing tables. The schema name records this first storage version. Reapplying
+the script fails at the existing schema and rolls back; it does not silently
+accept a different schema layout. Future migrations need their own reviewed
+versioned scripts. Do not run the historical `POSTGRESQL_Setup.sh`.
+
+Grant the writer role `USAGE` on `dashboard_v1` and `SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE` on
+`dashboard_v1.snapshot`. Grant the API role `USAGE` on the schema and `SELECT` on
+the table. Neither needs schema ownership, schema creation, DELETE or access to
+historical tables. Provision passwords in protected files using the required
+`DB_*` settings. The applications do not create databases, schemas or roles.
+
+There is one row, enforced by its boolean primary key and constraint. Its full
+timestamp must be finite, and its JSON must contain a node array and chart object.
+API block and information fields are derived when reading; there are no separate
+block/info columns or a second copy of each node's report. This store is a current
+view, not an append-only history service.
+
+## Required sampling settings
+
+| Process | Setting | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Persistence | `COLLECTOR_WS_URL` | Configured collector viewer URL ending in `/primus` |
+| Persistence | `SNAPSHOT_INTERVAL_MS` | Delay after a completed write or closed connection before the next sample/attempt |
+| Persistence | `SNAPSHOT_TIMEOUT_MS` | Deadline for connecting, or for receiving the requested node snapshot and a following chart message |
+| Persistence | `SNAPSHOT_MAX_BYTES` | Maximum bytes per incoming WebSocket frame/message |
+| Persistence | `SNAPSHOT_MAX_NODES` | Maximum distinct nodes in a snapshot |
+| API | `SNAPSHOT_MAX_AGE_MS` | Maximum age of the node observation before the API returns unavailable |
+
+All numeric settings are explicit positive integers up to 2147483647. There are
+no production defaults. Select bounded values for the deployment: the collector
+debounces chart generation, so the response deadline must allow that delay plus
+transport latency. The API age budget must allow the sample interval, chart wait,
+database write and tolerable outages. The local acceptance test uses 100 ms
+between samples, a 6000 ms response deadline, 65536 bytes/message, ten nodes and
+a 10000 ms API age limit; these are test settings, not a production profile.
+
+Each sampling cycle sends `emit: ["ready", {}]`, accepts its complete
+`emit: ["init", {nodes: ...}]`, then takes the first following chart message.
+Charts broadcast before the node reply and partial node events are ignored.
+Missing or invalid components discard the whole sample. Nodes and charts are
+separate collector messages; their contents do not represent an atomic chain
+state. Failure to receive charts prevents the node snapshot from becoming fresh.
+
+The writer handles Primus ping/pong messages. It retains at most one incoming
+node snapshot and one write, with no write queue. Both node and chart messages
+are individually size-limited, so the combined document can approach twice the
+per-message bound. Node IDs must be distinct and match the collector's permitted
+format; basic object/array validation is applied. Collector admission, field
+schemas, global connection/rate limits and deep payload review remain separate
+launch requirements.
+
+## Reconnect, writes and shutdown
+
+There is one sampling/deadline/retry timer. A new connection waits for the old
+socket's close event and any pending write to finish. Late messages from an old
+socket cannot affect a new connection. A slow database stops sampling instead
+of accumulating writes. SIGINT/SIGTERM stop sampling, close the socket, drain the
+pending write and close the pool; the WebSocket library completes its close
+handshake separately, using its bounded close timeout.
+
+Writes use SQL parameters and one `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT ... UPDATE`. An older
+observation cannot overwrite a newer row. If a database call fails, the writer
+closes the connection and requests a new complete snapshot after reconnecting.
+It does not replay the uncertain write. A delayed older commit is guarded by the
+stored observation timestamp. Run one writer against one collector/database;
+multi-writer election or clock coordination is not implemented. A backwards
+clock can prevent fresh writes, and future observations make the API unavailable;
+repair the clock rather than resetting stored history automatically.
+
+## Read API and freshness
+
+`/nodes` preserves the browser's array of rows with `id`, `utctime`, `block`,
+serialized `stats` and serialized `info`. These values come from the same
+stored document. `/blocks` and `/info` expose each node's reported block/info;
+they do not choose a canonical chain. `/charts` exposes the stored chart object.
+
+All four routes set `Cache-Control: no-store`. Successful responses include
+`X-Dashboard-Observed-At`. A missing, old or future snapshot, or a read failure,
+returns HTTP 503 with `{"error":"snapshot_unavailable"}` and no old payload or
+database error detail. The last stored snapshot is retained. A fresh empty
+collector snapshot returns empty nodes normally.
+
+The frontend still needs explicit stale/error rendering and removal of previously
+displayed cached nodes. An API freshness check does not by itself fix browser
+behavior or establish per-node freshness. The collector's `active` field is its
+cached status, not proof of recent transactions, block agreement or ticket health.
+
+## Acceptance scope
+
+`npm test` covers authentication, configuration, sampling lifecycle and all API
+views. `npm run test:persistence` requires the same fresh disposable PostgreSQL
+test environment as [database validation](postgres-validation.md). It creates
+the new schema and test roles, runs a real loopback collector, reports two
+distinct synthetic nodes, and exercises storage/API agreement, reconnect,
+disconnect, restricted grants, rejected older/invalid writes, and stale API
+behavior using an advanced test clock. It confirms existing sentinel data survives
+schema application and reapplication.
+
+No real efsn process, production database, validator key, browser build or TLS
+proxy is involved. Runtime/dependency review, collector resource limits, actual
+service supervision and complete browser/TLS acceptance remain open.
